1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,180
At the college fest, Aarav
whispers into a microphone.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:05,997
The speakers boom across the whole ground.

3
00:00:06,297 --> 00:00:10,029
Walking home, a street lamp
switches on by itself at dusk.

4
00:00:10,329 --> 00:00:12,171
Two very different jobs.

5
00:00:12,171 --> 00:00:14,078
One tiny part does both.

6
00:00:14,078 --> 00:00:15,189
A transistor.

7
00:00:15,689 --> 00:00:18,083
Think of a water tap on a big pipe.

8
00:00:18,433 --> 00:00:22,289
A small twist of the handle
controls a big flow of water.

9
00:00:22,639 --> 00:00:24,941
Close it fully, and no water flows.

10
00:00:24,941 --> 00:00:27,390
Open it fully, and you get full flow.

11
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:31,381
Wiggle the handle a little,
and the big flow wiggles too.

12
00:00:32,031 --> 00:00:34,471
A transistor is a tap for current.

13
00:00:34,471 --> 00:00:35,902
It has three legs.

14
00:00:36,252 --> 00:00:37,860
The base is the handle.

15
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:39,761
A tiny current goes in here.

16
00:00:40,111 --> 00:00:42,708
The collector is where
the big current comes in.

17
00:00:43,058 --> 00:00:45,425
The emitter is where both
currents leave together.

18
00:00:45,775 --> 00:00:48,984
A small base current controls
a big collector current.

19
00:00:48,984 --> 00:00:50,594
That is the whole trick.

20
00:00:51,244 --> 00:00:53,409
Let's build the switch on the breadboard.

21
00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:57,765
A five volt supply, a lamp,
and an n p n transistor.

22
00:00:58,115 --> 00:01:01,062
A resistor feeds the base
from an input switch.

23
00:01:01,412 --> 00:01:03,050
Input at zero volts.

24
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:04,797
No base current flows.

25
00:01:05,147 --> 00:01:07,145
So no collector current either.

26
00:01:07,145 --> 00:01:08,516
The lamp stays off.

27
00:01:08,516 --> 00:01:10,133
This is called cut off.

28
00:01:10,483 --> 00:01:12,809
Now flip the input to five volts.

29
00:01:12,809 --> 00:01:14,713
A small base current flows.

30
00:01:15,063 --> 00:01:16,175
The tap opens fully.

31
00:01:16,175 --> 00:01:18,548
Electrons rush through,
and the lamp lights up.

32
00:01:18,898 --> 00:01:21,607
This fully on state is called saturation.

33
00:01:21,607 --> 00:01:24,317
The transistor acts like a closed switch.

34
00:01:24,967 --> 00:01:26,119
Watch the scope.

35
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:29,105
When the input goes high,
the output goes low.

36
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:32,218
The output is taken across the transistor.

37
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:34,733
So this switch also flips the signal.

38
00:01:35,383 --> 00:01:36,693
Now the amplifier.

39
00:01:36,693 --> 00:01:39,530
We give the base a small, steady current.

40
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:41,644
The tap is now half open.

41
00:01:41,644 --> 00:01:43,445
The lamp glows half bright.

42
00:01:43,795 --> 00:01:47,557
Then we add a tiny wave from a
microphone, just ten millivolts.

43
00:01:47,907 --> 00:01:49,966
The base current wiggles a little.

44
00:01:49,966 --> 00:01:52,113
The collector current wiggles a lot.

45
00:01:52,463 --> 00:01:53,563
Watch the scope.

46
00:01:53,563 --> 00:01:56,092
The output wave is a hundred times bigger.

47
00:01:56,442 --> 00:01:57,628
But look closely.

48
00:01:57,628 --> 00:02:00,536
When the input goes up,
the output goes down.

49
00:02:00,886 --> 00:02:02,496
The output is upside down.

50
00:02:02,496 --> 00:02:05,143
We call this one eighty
degrees out of phase.

51
00:02:05,793 --> 00:02:09,334
So when is it a switch, and
when is it an amplifier?

52
00:02:09,684 --> 00:02:12,989
Plot the output voltage
against the input voltage.

53
00:02:13,339 --> 00:02:17,167
Below about zero point seven
volts, the base is shut.

54
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:18,857
The output stays high.

55
00:02:19,207 --> 00:02:21,024
That flat part is cut off.

56
00:02:21,024 --> 00:02:22,346
The switch is off.

57
00:02:22,696 --> 00:02:25,771
At a high input, the
transistor is fully on.

58
00:02:25,771 --> 00:02:27,612
The output is nearly zero.

59
00:02:27,962 --> 00:02:30,051
That flat part is saturation.

60
00:02:30,051 --> 00:02:31,450
The switch is on.

61
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,384
In between is the steep active region.

62
00:02:34,384 --> 00:02:37,541
A small input change
makes a big output change.

63
00:02:38,191 --> 00:02:39,924
A switch at the two ends.

64
00:02:39,924 --> 00:02:41,695
An amplifier in the middle.

65
00:02:42,495 --> 00:02:45,185
Now the maths, one small step at a time.

66
00:02:45,535 --> 00:02:47,941
Both currents leave through the emitter.

67
00:02:47,941 --> 00:02:49,914
So IE equals IB plus IC.

68
00:02:50,614 --> 00:02:53,882
The collector current is
beta times the base current.

69
00:02:53,882 --> 00:02:55,591
Beta is the current gain.

70
00:02:56,291 --> 00:03:00,780
The output is the supply, minus
the drop on the collector resistor.

71
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:05,613
So V out equals VCC, minus IC times RC.

72
00:03:06,313 --> 00:03:09,298
Divide the output change
by the input change.

73
00:03:09,298 --> 00:03:11,102
That is the voltage gain.

74
00:03:11,802 --> 00:03:17,935
AV equals minus beta, times RC,
divided by R in, the input resistance.

75
00:03:18,635 --> 00:03:19,929
Beta has no unit.

76
00:03:19,929 --> 00:03:22,024
It is a ratio of two currents.

77
00:03:22,024 --> 00:03:24,206
The minus sign means upside down.

78
00:03:24,856 --> 00:03:25,869
Worked example.

79
00:03:25,869 --> 00:03:27,316
Is the lamp fully on?

80
00:03:27,666 --> 00:03:29,605
The supply is five volts.

81
00:03:29,605 --> 00:03:31,609
The lamp is one kilo ohm.

82
00:03:31,609 --> 00:03:33,214
Beta is one hundred.

83
00:03:33,564 --> 00:03:38,223
The input is five volts, through
a base resistor of ten kilo ohms.

84
00:03:38,573 --> 00:03:43,519
The base current is five minus zero
point seven, over ten kilo ohms.

85
00:03:44,219 --> 00:03:46,112
That is zero point four
three milliamperes.

86
00:03:46,812 --> 00:03:49,221
Beta times this asks for
forty three milliamperes.

87
00:03:49,921 --> 00:03:53,911
But the lamp circuit can carry at
most five volts over one kilo ohm.

88
00:03:53,911 --> 00:03:55,502
That is five milliamperes.

89
00:03:56,202 --> 00:03:59,331
The base asks for more
than the circuit can give.

90
00:03:59,331 --> 00:04:02,991
So the transistor is saturated,
and the lamp is fully on.

91
00:04:03,641 --> 00:04:05,105
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:05,455 --> 00:04:09,250
Many students think the amplified
output moves in step with the input.

93
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,226
In a common emitter
amplifier, it does not.

94
00:04:12,226 --> 00:04:14,950
The output is one eighty
degrees out of phase.

95
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:18,083
And the transistor does not make energy.

96
00:04:18,083 --> 00:04:20,474
The extra power comes from the supply.

97
00:04:20,824 --> 00:04:22,448
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:22,448 --> 00:04:24,312
In PUC board papers.

99
00:04:24,962 --> 00:04:29,090
Draw the circuit, explain the switch
and the amplifier, and find the currents

100
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:30,003
and the gain.

101
00:04:30,653 --> 00:04:31,553
Try this.

102
00:04:31,532 --> 00:04:36,491
A common emitter amplifier has beta
fifty, an input resistance of one kilo

103
00:04:36,491 --> 00:04:40,121
ohm, and a collector
resistance of five kilo ohms.

104
00:04:40,121 --> 00:04:42,435
A ten millivolt signal goes in.

105
00:04:42,435 --> 00:04:43,699
What comes out?

106
00:04:49,099 --> 00:04:49,999
C.

107
00:04:49,455 --> 00:04:52,622
Two point five volts, one
eighty degrees out of phase.

108
00:04:52,972 --> 00:04:57,186
The voltage gain is beta
times RC, divided by R in.

109
00:04:57,536 --> 00:04:59,788
Fifty times five, divided by one.

110
00:04:59,788 --> 00:05:02,451
That is a gain of two hundred and fifty.

111
00:05:02,801 --> 00:05:06,788
Two hundred and fifty times ten
millivolts is two point five volts.

112
00:05:06,788 --> 00:05:08,735
And the output is upside down.

113
00:05:09,385 --> 00:05:10,932
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:14,132 --> 00:05:15,476
Next, number systems.

115
00:05:15,476 --> 00:05:17,970
Why do computers count
with only two digits?
